# Patches authored in this repo, applied LAST -- after the device stack and
# after the OGC block.
#
# This is the one place where .patch files are committed rather than fetched,
# because a patch written here has no upstream to be fetched from. Everything
# else in patches/ is gitignored.
#
# Rules of the road:
#
#   - Applied last on purpose. A local patch almost always adjusts something an
#     upstream patch already put in the tree, so it has to sit on top. If one
#     genuinely must land earlier, that is a signal it belongs in the device
#     series instead -- or that it should be sent to armada.
#
#   - Every entry needs a matching note in the "Local divergence" section of
#     docs/PATCHES.md saying why it is not upstreamable and what would let us
#     drop it. Without that note this directory quietly becomes a vendor fork
#     over a few kernel bumps, which is exactly what the rest of this repo is
#     built to avoid.
#
#   - These break first on a mainline bump, because nobody upstream is rebasing
#     them for you. The build reports local failures separately from upstream
#     ones for that reason.
#
# One entry today. It is NOT authored here, which stretches the heading above:
# it is committed because there is no RELIABLE source to fetch it from, not
# because nobody upstream wrote it. That distinction is the whole reason this
# directory is allowed to exist -- see docs/PATCHES.md, "Local divergence".

# 8-bpc output dithering on the Odin 3 panel.
#
#   what   adds `case 8:` to _dpu_encoder_setup_dither() in dpu_encoder.c,
#          enabling the same static ordered dither matrix the 6-bpc path uses.
#          Guarded by of_machine_is_compatible("ayn,odin3"), so every other
#          machine keeps today's behaviour exactly.
#
#   why    the ICNA35XX panel reports 8 bpc (patch 0028 sets .bpc = 8, and the
#          driver assigns it to display_info.bpc), and the switch has no case
#          for 8 -- so the default arm DISABLES dithering on this device today.
#          Gamescope composites HDR into the panel's Gamma 2.2 encoding and
#          scales SDR white, both of which produce smooth gradients that band
#          on 8 bits. This is the mitigation for that, and it is independent of
#          the AutoHDR work it was originally bundled with.
#
#   from   enjihn/armada-packages, kernel/patches/0011-...patch at commit
#          ee7c31c6fba444423e64fa12be370dc57fa014f7 -- a DRAFT pull request
#          against a fork (virtudude/armada-packages#13), never merged to
#          armada-os/armada-packages. That is why fetch-patches.sh cannot get
#          it: this repo pins ARMADA_REF against the real armada-packages, and
#          the patch is not there.
#
#   note   NOT byte-for-byte theirs, and deliberately so. Their patch is a
#          zero-context (-U0) diff: line numbers, and nothing to match them
#          against. Tested -- against a tree with two lines added above the
#          function, it exits 0 and inserts `case 8:` INSIDE `case 6:`, which
#          still compiles and quietly breaks 6-bpc panels. patches/0010-msm-
#          resource-cleanup.patch edits this same file, so that shift is not
#          hypothetical. Regenerated here with three lines of context, which on
#          the same test applies at the right place and says so
#          ("Hunk #2 succeeded at 404 (offset 2 lines)").
#
#          Their original is sha256
#          f56266556535d3e8f28643232db779f7f68de55a00cb36b86a1ecf166816ab56;
#          re-fetch it and compare the hunk bodies to confirm this is the same
#          change. The commit message in the file is theirs, unedited.
#
#   UNVERIFIED ON HARDWARE. Whether the banding it fixes is visible on this
#   panel, and whether the dither noise it adds is preferable, is a judgement
#   nobody here has been able to make yet. There is no runtime toggle: changing
#   your mind means rebuilding the kernel.
0001-drm-msm-dpu-enable-8bpc-dither-on-ayn-odin3.patch
